s unresolved and active, but H30 is a more informative validation target than H13 given its direction-aligned interval and larger relative point estimate.\n\nThe new population-level screens H31-H33 were null or opposite for their pre-specified stronger-dependency claims. H31 has upper bound 0.0243 versus threshold approximately 0.0260; H32 has upper bound 0.0215 versus threshold 0.0255; H33 has upper bound 0.0051 versus threshold 0.0252. In each case, even the direction-aligned confidence bound falls below the 10% clinical threshold, so these are rejected and closed.\n\nThe untested broad screens H15-H19 were also rejected and closed. Their upper practical bounds are below the 10% thresholds, or the point estimates are opposite to the pre-specified direction: H15 upper 0.0169 < 0.0256; H16 upper 0.0068 < 0.0282; H17 upper 0.0213 < 0.0253; H18 upper 0.0231 < 0.0301; H19 upper 0.0319 < 0.0415. None provides support for a clinically meaningful association in its registered direction.\n\nThe overall evidence remains exploratory. No predictor should be treated causally or generalizably at this stage. Feature names are opaque, and the H30 subgroup test was one of several pre-specified refinements, so multiplicity remains relevant. Independent validation of H30 is the most useful next step because a replicated interval that stays above null and ideally above the 0.0250 clinical threshold would materially change the assessment of feature_021 within the feature_034=1 stratum.",
